      "role": "user",
      "content": "That's an interesting way to put it, but I need to move away from the metaphors. I am writing a formal legal memorandum for a client. \n\nPlease provide a precise, technical comparison of the two. Specifically, how does the requirement of \"impossibility\" differ between a force majeure clause (which is a matter of contractual interpretation) and the doctrine of frustration (which is a matter of common law)? I need to know the specific legal thresholds for each.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a formal legal memorandum, it is essential to delineate the technical distinctions between the \"force majeure\" clause and the common law \"doctrine of frustration\" with respect to their application in the context of unexpected business interruptions.\n\nA \"force majeure\" clause is a contractual provision that parties agree upon at the outset of their agreement. It typically enumerates specific events that, if they occur, will excuse one or both parties from performing their contractual obligations. The interpretation of such clauses is generally governed by the language of the contract itself. The threshold for invoking a force majeure clause is the occurrence of the specified events, which must be beyond the control of the parties and not due to their own fault. The clause may also require that the party invoking it take reasonable steps to mitigate the effects of the force majeure event.\n\nIn contrast, the common law \"doctrine of frustration\" does not rely on the parties' agreement but is instead a judicially created remedy that applies when an unforeseen event occurs after the formation of the contract, rendering the performance of the contract impossible, illegal, or radically different from what was contemplated by the parties at the time of the contract's execution. The threshold for frustration is higher than that for a force majeure clause; it requires that",
      "source": "target",
