      "content": "That covers the high-level distinction. To finish my analysis, I want to look at the \"unpredictability\" aspect of these events. \n\nIn cases where a disruption isn't just a single catastrophic event, but rather a series of small, mounting, and somewhat chaotic incidents that eventually make business operations impossible—effectively a \"death by a thousand cuts\"—how would a court evaluate that cumulative chaos? Does that kind of compounding, unpredictable disruption settle more comfortably under the doctrine of frustration, or can it be captured by a well-drafted force majeure clause?",
      "source": "evaluator"
